  pm-wrbe,discovered_from,"2026-08-10T12:11:45.027Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
body: "## Reproduction\n\nMeasured 2026-08-10 on a workspace where `changelog:pm:check` reported `changed: false`.\n\n1. Add one typed dependency row to one long-closed item (`pm update pm-02c4 --dep \"id=pm-qwoy,kind=implements\"`).\n2. Run `changelog:pm:check`.\n\nThe check exits 1 with:\n\n```\nWhat happened:\n  Changelog is out of date: CHANGELOG.md\nWhat is required:\n  Adjust command input or tracker state and retry.\n```\n\nThat is the entire diagnostic. The recovery bundle repeats the command line and the provided flag names. Nothing states which release section changed, which items moved, whether any item entered or left the changelog, or whether the byte count changed.\n\n## Why the omission is expensive\n\nThe agent cannot tell the two possible causes apart, and they demand opposite responses:\n\n- **Membership change** — an item moved between release sections. That is a real attribution defect and must be investigated before regenerating.\n- **Intra-section reordering** — the same items in the same sections, in a different order, because rows sort on a mutable timestamp (pm-nwuzaq). That is cosmetic and is healed by regenerating in the same commit.\n\nDistinguishing them requires the agent to generate a second copy to a scratch path and diff it by hand. In this measurement that hand diff was three lines and settled the question immediately: `item_count` 2017 and `bytes` 355556 were identical before and after, and one row moved from line 2571 to 2568. The check already computed everything needed to say so and discarded it.\n\n## Consequence for the convention this sustains\n\nThis is the instrument behind the standing \"do not touch closed items\" convention. Because the check cannot say that a diff is order-only, every closed-item mutation looks equally dangerous, so the whole terminal-record enrichment programme has been deferred behind it. pm-wrbe records this deferral as the single largest unlock in the backlog and attributes it to changelog re-bucketing. The measurement above shows re-bucketing is no longer what happens: release-tag attribution holds section membership stable, and only row order moves. The convention outlived its cause because the diagnostic could not report the difference.\n\n## Required shape\n\nThe check is a comparison; it already holds both sides. It should report the comparison, bounded like every other read: counts first, then a capped sample of moved rows, with the section each row moved within or between, and an explicit flag for whether membership changed at all."
